WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.124
SQL is older than the personal computer.

00:00:06.124 --> 00:00:10.248
It was designed in 1974, and almost every job in tech and business still uses it today, in 2026.

00:00:10.248 --> 00:00:14.371
The core ideas have held for fifty years, while the language keeps evolving to meet new needs.

00:00:14.371 --> 00:00:18.495
Your career path does not matter.

00:00:18.495 --> 00:00:22.619
SQL is on it.

00:00:22.619 --> 00:00:26.956
You write SQL if you are an analyst, looking at sales numbers.

00:00:26.956 --> 00:00:31.294
You write SQL if you are a data scientist, training a model.

00:00:31.294 --> 00:00:35.631
You write SQL if you are an engineer, shipping a product.

00:00:35.631 --> 00:00:39.969
You write SQL if you are a founder, looking at your own dashboard.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:44.306
Four different jobs, same query language.

00:00:44.306 --> 00:00:47.526
There are four common paths through this course.

00:00:47.526 --> 00:00:50.746
Analyst, the person who answers business questions with data.

00:00:50.746 --> 00:00:53.965
Scientist, who builds the models.

00:00:53.965 --> 00:00:57.185
Engineer, who builds the systems that hold the data.

00:00:57.185 --> 00:01:00.405
And founder, who needs to do all three at once.

00:01:00.405 --> 00:01:03.625
Each path uses different parts of the course more than others.

00:01:03.625 --> 00:01:08.165
Wherever you are headed, SQL is going to show up.

00:01:08.165 --> 00:01:12.704
The next ten weeks teach you the language and the systems beneath it.

00:01:12.704 --> 00:01:17.243
The principles are the same as they were fifty years ago, and the language keeps evolving to meet current needs.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:21.783
Get this right and you have a tool that travels with you for the rest of your career.
